titre $Key to the clitocyboid - tricholomatoid species- Gro Gulden
com$Gro Gulden A preliminary guide to the macromycetes in the Finse area / GF 2011


1,2 $Pileus and stipe with reddish brown colour shades: liver-brown, purplish brown or brick-red
1,4 $Fruitbody whitish, pinkish, beige, grey, grey-brown to blackish
2,0 $Fruitbody small, thin fleshed, pileus 1-4 cm, depressed to umbilicate, when moist with ± translucently striate margin $Omphalina rivulicola
2,3 $Fruitbody fleshy, pileus 2-12 cm, not translucently striate
3,0 $Pileus 2-5 cm, in Dryas heaths, smell absent, spores acyanophilic, single in preparations $Clitocybe lateritia
3,0 $Pileus 5-12 cm, on disturbed ground (mostly man-made habitats), smell pleasant, spores cyanophilic, finely verruculose, mostly adhering in tetrades in preparations $Lepista multiformis
4,5 $Fruitbody whitish to pale pinkish, hygrophanous or not
4,7 $Fruitbody more dull, beige, pale or darker grey- brown, grey to blackish, hygrophanous
5,0 $Pileus not 'frosty' but with a white pubescent margin, pinkish to pale flesh-brown, smell sweetish $Clitocybe marginella
5,6 $Pileus ± 'frosty', whitish, smell indistinct to somewhat acidic
6,0 $Fruitbody medium sized, pileus 2-10 cm, stipe 0.3-1.5 cm thick, mostly (sub)fasciculate, flesh lilac with FeSO4, not hygrophanous, spores cyanophilic, basidia siderophilous; $Lyophyllum connatum
6,0 $Fruitbody small, pileus 1-4 cm, stipe 0.2-0.4 cm thick, hygrophanous, spores acyanophilic and basidia not siderophilous $Clitocybe candicans var. dryadicola
7,0 $Lamellae emarginate to shortly decurrent, stipe base ± bulbose $Melanoleuca stridula
7,8 $Lamellae decurrent, stipe cylindric to clavate, not bulbose at base
8,0 $Smell and taste distinctly farinaceous, pileus dark brown-grey, margin pruinose and not translucently striate, spores small, almost globose $Clitocybe ditopa
8,9 $Smell and taste not distinctly farinaceous but indistinct or sweetish, spores ellipsoid
9,0 $Pileus deeply depressed, blackish brown, margin not striate, stipe slender, ± clavate $Clitocybe concava
9,10 $Pileus convex to depressed, beige to grey-brown to medium chocolate-brown, stipe ± cylindric
10,0 $Smell sweetish, pileus medium dark grey-brown, not 'frosty', lamellae whitish, spores ellipsoid 6.7-8(-9.5) x 3.5-4.5 µm, cyanophilic $Clitocybe harmajae
10,11 $Smell not sweetish, taste none or ± farinaceous, pileus pale beige to grey-brown
11,0 $Pileus shallowly depressed, often papillate, margin ± striate, smell/taste ± farinaceous, spore print white $Clitocybe vibecina
11,0 $Pileus margin not striate, pileus ± 'frosty' and remaining so at margin, smell/taste not farinaceous, spore print yellow $Clitocybe festiva

15,0$The lamellae in this group of genera vary from emarginate to decurrent and the stipes are ± fleshy (not cartilaginous, hollow or filiform). In general, the species in this group of genera are large and more robust than the species of the other genera of Tricholomataceae. However, in the mountains species with rather small fruitbodies, especially whitish Clitocybe species, are often seen.///Included genera: Clitocybe, Lepista, and Melanoleuca
